@chapter 6 | Heat and Thermodynamics

@intro
Heat questions are about daily life: steam burns, thermometers and temperature scales, expansion, refrigerators and air-conditioners, **humidity and evaporation** (earthen pots, drying clothes, sweating), **boiling point and pressure** (hills and pressure cookers), and **conduction, convection and radiation** (thermos flask, white clothes, cloudy nights, woollens). It contains **{pyqs} PYQs and {ones} one-liners**.
@end

@section 6.1 | Latent Heat and Temperature Scales

@q 6:1

**Latent heat** is heat absorbed or released during a change of state without any change of temperature. Steam at 100°C carries an extra **540 cal/g** (2,260 J/g, latent heat of vaporisation) compared with water at 100°C, and gives it all up on condensing on the skin, so a steam burn is worse. Ice needs **80 cal/g** (latent heat of fusion) to melt.

@src 6:2
@pyq U.P.P.C.S. (Pre) 2024
Which of the following is a correct relationship between Fahrenheit (°F) and Celsius (°C) degrees?
(a) °C = (9/5)°F + 32
(b) °C = °F – 273.15
(c) °F = (9/5)°C + 32
(d) °F = °C – 273.15
@ans (c)
@end

@q 6:3-5

@src 6:6
@pyq U.P. P.C.S. (Mains) 2016
Let the average temperatures in Centigrade (C) and Fahrenheit (F) be C_{av} and F_{av}. If C and F are related to F = (9/5)C + 32, then F_{av} and C_{av} have the relation : *(the source writes the averages with a bar)*
(a) F_{av} = (9/5)C_{av} + 32
(b) F_{av} = C_{av} + 32
(c) F_{av} = (9/5)C_{av}
(d) F_{av} = (9/5)C_{av} – 32
@ans (a)
@end

@table
Point | Celsius | Fahrenheit | Kelvin
Absolute zero | -273.15 | -459.67 | 0
**Same reading on C and F** | **-40** | **-40** | 233.15
Ice melts | 0 | 32 | 273.15
**Water densest** | 4 | 39.2 | 277.15
Normal body temperature | **37** | **98.6** (often given as 98.4) | 310
Water boils (sea level) | 100 | 212 | 373.15
@end

Conversion: **C/5 = (F - 32)/9 = (K - 273)/5**. Because the relation is linear, it holds for **averages** too. 98.4°F works out to 36.89°C and 200°F to 93.3°C.

@q 6:7-8

**Alcohol** freezes at -115°C (mercury at -39°C), so it is used in thermometers for very cold places; mercury is better for high temperatures (boils at 357°C). **Ethylene glycol** is the **antifreeze** in car radiators: added to water it lowers the freezing point and raises the boiling point.

@section 6.2 | Expansion and Cooling Devices

@q 6:9-10

@src 6:11
@pyq U.P.P.C.S. (Pre) 2012
When ice melts, then
(a) Volume increases
(b) Volume decreases
(c) Mass increases
(d) Mass decreases
@ans (b)
@end

On heating, a body expands **in every direction** (length, breadth and volume), so its **density falls** but mass and weight stay the same. Ice is the exception at 0°C: water **contracts** when ice melts, so volume decreases. Gaps are left in railway tracks and bridges, and telephone wires sag in summer, to allow for expansion.

@q 6:12

A **thermostat** (often a bimetallic strip of two metals that expand differently) switches heating or cooling on and off to hold a set temperature; it is used in irons, refrigerators, geysers and ovens.

@q 6:13-14

Food is safest at **about 4°C**, below which most bacteria grow very slowly ("danger zone" 5-60°C). A refrigerator's cold coils condense and remove water vapour, so the air inside is **dry**; soggy biscuits lose moisture and turn crisp.

@q 6:15-16

@q 6:17

An **air-conditioner** controls temperature, **humidity** and air circulation; a desert **air cooler** cools by evaporating water, which **adds** humidity, so it works poorly in humid weather.

@section 6.3 | Humidity and Evaporation

@q 6:18

**Relative humidity** = actual water vapour / maximum the air can hold at that temperature. Warm air can hold more vapour, so with the same moisture, RH **falls** as temperature rises. Absolute humidity rises with more evaporation. Both statements are true, but the second does not explain the first.

@src 6:19
@pyq U.P.P.C.S. (Pre) 2008
In which of the following conditions will wet clothes dry earliest?
(a) 100% RH, 60°C temperature
(b) 100% RH, 20°C temperature
(c) 20% RH, 20°C temperature
(d) 20% RH, 60°C temperature
@ans (d)
@end

**Evaporation** is faster with **higher temperature, lower humidity, more wind and larger surface area**, and it **cools** the surface because the escaping molecules take latent heat with them.

@q 6:27

@src 6:25
@pyq U.P. B.E.O. (Pre) 2019
Loss of water from the body through perspiration depends on :
(a) Atmospheric temperature only
(b) Atmospheric humidity only
(c) Both (a) and (b)
(d) None of (a) and (b)
@ans (c)
@end

@q 6:26

Sweat cools us by **evaporating**. On a hot, **humid** day sweat cannot evaporate fast, so we feel sticky and uncomfortable; this is a true fact but not the reason why evaporation cools, so (R) does not explain (A). The **heat index** combines temperature and humidity to express how hot it feels.

@q 6:28-29

@section 6.4 | Pressure and Boiling Point

@q 6:30

@q 6:31-34

@table
Change | Boiling point | Example
**Lower pressure** (high altitude) | Falls (about 1°C per 300 m) | Water boils near 70°C on Everest; food cooks slowly in hills
**Higher pressure** | Rises | **Pressure cooker**: about 120°C, so food cooks faster
**Dissolved impurity** (salt, sugar) | **Rises** | Salted water boils above 100°C
Dissolved impurity | Freezing point **falls** | Salt on icy roads; salt-ice mixture down to about -22°C
@end

The maximum pressure, and so the temperature, inside a cooker is set by the **weight on the vent and the area of the hole** it sits on (pressure = force/area).

