@section 6.5 | Conduction, Convection and Radiation

@src 6:21
@pyq U.P.P.C.S. (Pre) 2019
A liquid remains hot or cold for a long time in thermos flask because there is no loss or gain of heat by
(a) Conduction
(b) Convection and radiation
(c) Both (a) and (b)
(d) None of the above
@ans (c)
@end

A **thermos (Dewar) flask** has a double glass wall with a **vacuum** between (stops conduction and convection), **silvered** surfaces (reflect radiation) and a cork stopper and supports that conduct poorly.

@q 6:22-24

**White** surfaces reflect most radiation and **black** surfaces absorb (and emit) most. An umbrella that is **white on top** reflects sunlight, and **black underneath** absorbs heat rising from the ground instead of reflecting it onto the user. Light clothes suit summer; dark clothes suit winter.

@q 6:36-37

Clouds act like a blanket: they absorb the heat the ground radiates at night and send part of it back, so **cloudy nights are warmer** and frost forms on clear, calm nights. **Albedo** (reflectivity) is highest for **fresh snow (80-90%)**, then desert sand (30-40%), and low for forests, crops and oceans.

@q 6:38

@q 6:39

Wool keeps us warm because its fibres trap **air**, a very poor conductor; the protein itself is not the reason, so (R) is false. A quilt warms for the same reason, and double-glazed windows and hollow bricks insulate with trapped air.

@trap
Steam burns are worse because of **latent heat (540 cal/g)**. **-40° is the same on C and F.** Ice **decreases** in volume on melting. Wet clothes dry fastest at **high temperature and low humidity**. Salt **raises** the boiling point and **lowers** the freezing point. Boiling point **falls** at altitude because **pressure falls**. Wool is warm because of **trapped air**, not protein. A cooler adds humidity; an AC removes it.
@end

@heatmap

@pattern
Heat questions are explanatory and heavily repeated (steam burns, pressure cooker, hill cooking, earthen pot, white clothes, thermos). **Assertion-Reason** is now the favourite format: the 2010, 2016, 2023, 2024 and 2025 papers each had one, often testing whether a true reason actually *explains* the assertion. Temperature conversion returned in 2015, 2016, 2019 and 2024.
@end

